@using Geef.Atelier.Web.Components.UI.Orders

<section class="lp-section lp-caps" id="rely" aria-labelledby="lp-caps-title" data-testid="landing-rely">
    <div class="lp-container">
        <div class="lp-split-head">
            <div>
                <p class="lp-eyebrow">What you can count on</p>
                <h2 class="lp-h2" id="lp-caps-title">What it does for you, and what it does not.</h2>
            </div>
            <p class="lp-lede">
                A text that reads well can still be wrong. Atelier is built to show you where it may be: what it was given, what it had
                to guess and what it checked.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="caps-grid">
            <div class="cap" data-testid="landing-cap-files">
                <div class="ico" aria-hidden="true"><LandingIllustrations Name="CapFiles" /></div>
                <h3>Your files, in full</h3>
                <p>Up to @Features.FullTextChars.Grouped() characters (roughly @Features.FullTextChars.ApproxPages() pages), the writer gets your files completely; nothing is cut off or shortened first. If it seems to have read only parts, the result says so.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="cap" data-testid="landing-cap-questions">
                <div class="ico" aria-hidden="true"><LandingIllustrations Name="CapQuestions" /></div>
                @if (Features.Questions)
                {
                    <h3>It asks first, not afterwards</h3>
                    <p>It is built to ask only what changes the result, before it starts and never while it writes. What it does not ask is written down as an assumption.</p>
                }
                else
                {
                    <h3>Guesses are written down</h3>
                    <p>What you did not say and it had to assume is listed with the text, so you can see it and correct it.</p>
                }
            </div>
            <div class="cap" data-testid="landing-cap-gaps">
                <div class="ico" aria-hidden="true"><LandingIllustrations Name="CapGaps" /></div>
                <h3>Gaps stay visible</h3>
                <p>The writer is asked to leave a gap in [square brackets] where a fact is missing, such as a date or a name. The result lists every gap to fill in before you send the text.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="cap" data-testid="landing-cap-checked">
                <div class="ico" aria-hidden="true"><LandingIllustrations Name="CapChecked" /></div>
                <h3>It says what was checked</h3>
                <p>Every text written the standard way comes with what was checked, what is still open and what was assumed. It never claims that the text is right.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="cap" data-testid="landing-cap-versions">
                <div class="ico" aria-hidden="true"><LandingIllustrations Name="CapVersions" /></div>
                @if (Features.ChangeText)
                {
                    <h3>Change it in your words</h3>
                    <p>Say what to change and you get a new version; the earlier ones are kept, up to @Features.MaxVersions in all. Every request stays under My work.</p>
                }
                else
                {
                    <h3>Everything in one place</h3>
                    <p>Every request, its text and the downloads stay under My work until you delete them.</p>
                }
            </div>
            <div class="cap" data-testid="landing-cap-export">
                <div class="ico" aria-hidden="true"><LandingIllustrations Name="CapExport" /></div>
                <h3>Ready to download</h3>
                <p>Download every text as Word, PDF or Markdown. The file holds only the text; the open points stay on the page.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="lp-limits" data-testid="landing-limits">
            <h3 class="lp-limits-title">What Atelier does not do</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>It can still be wrong. The checks look for certain problems; they do not prove that everything in the text is right.</li>
                @if (Features.FactCheck != FactCheckOffer.NotShown)
                {
                    <li data-testid="landing-limit-factcheck">The fact check can miss things, and it often points at harmless sentences too, such as a closing line.</li>
                }
                <li>It does not send your text on to other people. You read it, fill in the gaps and decide what to send.</li>
                <li>It is not a chat. Once it starts, you wait for the result, and you cannot add files to a request later.</li>
                @if (Features.WebResearch)
                {
                    <li data-testid="landing-limit-web">It looks things up on the web only for gaps in your request, never when you add files, and it never puts your files into a search.</li>
                }
                else
                {
                    <li data-testid="landing-limit-web">Right now it does not look things up on the web for your texts. It works from what you give it.</li>
                }
                @if (!Features.LearnsFromEarlierTexts)
                {
                    <li data-testid="landing-limit-learning">It does not learn from your earlier texts. Only your About me details carry over.</li>
                }
                <li>It cannot read scanned pages (photos of paper).</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="lp-limits-more"><a href="/how-it-works#why" data-enhance-nav="false">More on what it does and does not do</a></p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

@code {
    /// <summary>What the start page may call available for the viewer.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public HowItWorksFeatures Features { get; set; } = null!;
}
